WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.620
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gold standard;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.100
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.800
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.900
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
I do!

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.160
Today we hope to settle
the debate once and for all

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.000
of the role of cholesterol
in heart disease.

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:33.600
The cholesterol controversy is over.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.740
In fact, you can argue
it was over a century ago.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.240
It seemed obvious in 1920 that
high cholesterol levels in the blood

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.920
infiltrating your artery walls was
the cause of coronary heart disease,

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.180
the #1 killer of men and women,
confirmed as unequivocally

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.360
as the revelation that blood
circulated throughout the body

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.440
or that the tuberculosis
bacteria causes tuberculosis.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.420
The question is why
did it take so long?

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.600
What is so puzzling is why
we have to work so hard

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.180
to sell the message
given what seems to be

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.160
an unbeatable amount
of hard evidence.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.060
Many rejected the cholesterol-
heart disease link because

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.060
so many patients were
dying of coronary heart disease

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.120
despite so-called perfectly
normal cholesterol levels.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.100
Of course as I've detailed before,
having normal cholesterol levels

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.700
in a society where it's normal
to drop dead of a heart attack

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.560
isn't exactly saying much.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.000
Ideally, we want to get our total
cholesterol well under 150,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.280
since having high cholesterol
levels in your blood

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.660
is thought of as the only direct
atherosclerotic risk factor.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.600
All the other things -- smoking,
high blood pressure, diabetes,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.740
inactivity, obesity --

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.760
just exacerbate the damage
caused by the high cholesterol.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.660
Another factor may be the
preoccupation of cardiologists

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.240
with all the new fancy gadgets
and procedures out there.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.580
It's like we train them
to be highly-skilled,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.100
high-tech fighter pilots
to fight a war,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.120
but then sent them on some boring
preventive diplomatic mission.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.760
But the reasons may be
even more personal than that.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.480
As an editorial in the Journal of
the American Heart Association

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.880
asked nearly 50 years ago,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.200
why do we pretend the cause
of heart disease is mysterious?

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.280
There is no mystery as to why
the incidence of heart disease,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.000
like that of lung disease
and of venereal disease --

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.290
sexually-transmitted disease --

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.160
continues to rise for many decades
after the cause is established.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.210
Why?

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.120
Because human beings,
including physicians,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.860
are eager for excuses,
not to face annoying facts,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.820
and so they continue to do things
which are agreeable but hazardous.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.020
People tend to reject new ideas,
even when they don't impose

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.900
any change in our way of life,
and it's almost impossible

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.140
for most men to accept any
suggestion that it might be wise

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.860
to give up agreeable habits
such as smoking, unsafe sex,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.200
or eating their favorite foods.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.760
This continuing challenge is
represented by a senior professor

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.880
of medicine who questioned whether
an alteration of diet would really

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.500
affect the course of heart disease.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.260
These professors know the facts.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.720
The problem is that they,
like so many patients,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.300
will not allow themselves
to believe the message.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.840
Eating rich food just means
too much to too many people,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.340
even when it's our
gravest mortal threat.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.840
Scientific consensus panels going
back decades established

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.840
beyond a reasonable doubt
that lowering LDL cholesterol

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.940
reduces the risk of heart attacks.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.440
Consistent evidence
unequivocally establishes that LDL

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.240
causes our #1 killer, atherosclerotic
cardiovascular disease.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.280
And what raises LDL cholesterol?

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.420
Saturated fat.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.600
And where is cholesterol-raising
saturated fat found?

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.380
The #1 source is dairy,
the #2 source is chicken,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.380
then pastries, pork, and burgers.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.120
And it's not just saturated fat.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.520
Dietary cholesterol has been known

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.200
as a dominant factor in the genesis
of atherosclerosis since 1908,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.560
which is why we should lower
our intake of saturated fats,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.220
trans fats and dietary cholesterol
as much as possible.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.700
This is consistent with
how our biology evolved.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.480
Extensive evidence clearly
indicates that a plant-based diet

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.960
was the traditional eating pattern
of our distant ancestors,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.740
and so dietary cholesterol intakes
were typically very low,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.740
while at the same time we were
packing in whole plant foods

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.820
containing components like fiber
to help us eliminate cholesterol.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.360
Where is dietary cholesterol
coming from now?

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.880
Overwhelmingly eggs,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.660
with the #2 source chicken,
then beef, dairy, and pork.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.520
So wait a second.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.080
If the Institute of Medicine

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.200
recommends that individuals
consume as little dietary

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.700
cholesterol as possible,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.740
presumably that would mean
cutting out foods like eggs entirely.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.640
But does eating cholesterol actually
raise your blood cholesterol?

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.300
We'll find out next.

00:05:10.802 --> 00:05:14.580
The question, does egg feeding
(in other words, dietary cholesterol)

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.860
affect the level of cholesterol in the
blood, was answered 40 years ago.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.260
Give someone half a cup of eggs
a day and within 2, 3, 4 weeks

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.200
their cholesterol keeps going up,
and then stop the eggs

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.400
by switching to an egg substitute
and the cholesterol comes down.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.520
Or start people on the egg
substitute and not much happens,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.440
but then start feeding eggs and
their cholesterol shoots right up.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:44.280
Put people on a cholesterol-free
diet and their cholesterols drop.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.160
But then add some
egg yolk cholesterol and

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:48.600
their cholesterol goes up.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.300
Take it away and their
cholesterol goes down.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.200
You could do this all year.
And it's within days:

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.680
10 days of eggs and
cholesterol shot up 50 points.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.500
Take the eggs away
and it comes back down.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:09.180
You can reproduce this effect
over and over and over and over,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:15.000
though evidently penguin omelets
are only about half as deadly.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.640
Switch people from
a high cholesterol diet

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.740
to a cholesterol free diet,
and you can drop

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.220
blood cholesterol levels
as much as a hundred points.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.420
OK, but that was giving people more
than a half cup of egg yolks a day.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:33.060
But even just a single egg a day can
increase people's LDL cholesterol 12%.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.960
Put all the studies together
in this 2020 meta-analysis,

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.380
more than 50 randomized
controlled trials feeding people eggs,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:46.380
and egg consumption significantly
increases LDL-cholesterol, period.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.940
Now studies funded by the Egg
Board use a variety of methods

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.940
to try to minimize the reported
negative health effects of eggs

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.360
by, for example, claiming that
dietary cholesterol affects

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.620
only certain people.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:04.380
That's actually been put to the
test, and only 3% were even

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.260
potentially "nonresponders"
to dietary cholesterol,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.520
and even these probably evidenced
some adverse response.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:17.340
Wait, then how was the Egg Board
able to design a study in which

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.100
egg intake did not change
blood cholesterol?

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.020
By feeding them sufficiently
large quantities of dairy and meat.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.880
See, there's a plateau effect.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.260
Though all lines of evidence converge
to indicate that dietary cholesterol

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.260
is a major factor in promoting
hardening of the arteries,

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.980
confusion about dietary
cholesterol has arisen because

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:41.760
above a certain ceiling you basically
max out cholesterol absorption.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.120
Check it out. If you're eating
a strictly plant-based diet

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.920
with a baseline of zero cholesterol
intake, and you start adding meat,

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.060
dairy or eggs to your diet,

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.880
you can get a dramatic
rise in blood cholesterol.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:59.400
But as your diet starts out more
and more meaty, you can saturate

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.700
your system and basically max
out on the additional effect.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.600
So no wonder the Egg Board
packed in the extra meat and dairy

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.520
to mask the egg effect.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.430
A systematic review of egg industry
"Funding and Cholesterol Research "

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.660
sought out to see if industry-funded
studies were more likely to report

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.920
conclusions that were not supported
by their own objective findings.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.540
Of the non industry-funded studies
on the effect of egg ingestion on

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:31.080
cholesterol, cholesterol increases
were reported in about 90%.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.480
Among industry-funded studies,

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:37.200
cholesterol increases were
reported in about 90%.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.800
In other words, even the egg
industry funded studies showed

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.460
eggs increased blood cholesterol,
and not a single study

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.500
funded by anyone
showed a significant decrease.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.320
Okay, but here's the crazy part.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.920
About half of the industry-funded
studies reported conclusions

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.200
that were discordant
with their own results.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.560
In other words, they found
that cholesterol went up,

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.800
but if you read their conclusions,
they exonerated eggs.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.500
That's why you can't
just read the abstract.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.420
You actually have to
see what they found.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.780
Readers, editors, and the
public should remain alert

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:15.000
to funding sources in interpreting
study findings and conclusions.

